            "summary": "(Added and updated sources for the wikiHaus content team.)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "sourcing",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#*The cartridge fuse is a cylindrical fuse that has been common in a wide variety of devices for many years, from homes to small electronic devices. They have metal contact or terminal points on either end and consist primarily of a tube that contains the wire.<ref>https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3483613/</ref>",
                    "#*The blade fuse is a common type of automotive fuse that has come into use in the last 20-30 years. They vaguely resemble the plug of a power cord, with two metal prongs emanating from a plastic housing that contains the wire. Previously, most vehicles also contained small glass cartridge fuses. Blade fuses plug conveniently into banks, and relatively little space is required to house a large number of them together.<ref>https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3483613/</ref>",
                    "#Turn the meter on and set it to [[Test Continuity with a Multimeter|measure continuity]]. Turn the dial on the multimeter so it points to the continuity setting, which looks like 5 curved vertical lines. Before you test the fuse, put the positive and negative leads together and listen for the meter to beep to ensure it’s working properly.<ref>https://www.swe-check.com.au/pages/learn_fuse_testing.php</ref>[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 6 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Put one lead on each end of the fuse and look at the display. Because the fuse is little more than a single wire—and no complex parts to worry about—it does not matter which side receives the positive or negative lead.<ref>https://www.swe-check.com.au/pages/learn_fuse_testing.php</ref>[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 7 Version 2.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#*The cartridge fuse is a cylindrical fuse that has been common in a wide variety of devices for many years, from homes to small electronic devices. They have metal contact or terminal points on either end and consist primarily of a tube that contains the wire.<ref>https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3483613/</ref>",
                    "#*The blade fuse is a common type of automotive fuse that has come into use in the last 20-30 years. They vaguely resemble the plug of a power cord, with two metal prongs emanating from a plastic housing that contains the wire. Previously, most vehicles also contained small glass cartridge fuses. Blade fuses plug conveniently into banks, and relatively little space is required to house a large number of them together.<ref> https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3483613/</ref>",
                    "#Turn the meter on and set it to [[Test Continuity with a Multimeter|measure continuity]]. Turn the dial on the multimeter so it points to the continuity setting, which looks like 5 curved vertical lines. Before you test the fuse, put the positive and negative leads together and listen for the meter to beep to ensure it’s working properly.[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 6 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Put one lead on each end of the fuse and look at the display. Because the fuse is little more than a single wire—and no complex parts to worry about—it does not matter which side receives the positive or negative lead.[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 7 Version 2.jpg|center]]"
                ]
            }
        },
