            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "sourcing",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#*The cartridge fuse is a cylindrical fuse that has been common in a wide variety of devices for many years, from homes to small electronic devices. They have metal contact or terminal points on either end and consist primarily of a tube that contains the wire.<ref>https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3483613/</ref>",
                    "#*The blade fuse is a common type of automotive fuse that has come into use in the last 20-30 years. They vaguely resemble the plug of a power cord, with two metal prongs emanating from a plastic housing that contains the wire. Previously, most vehicles also contained small glass cartridge fuses. Blade fuses plug conveniently into banks, and relatively little space is required to house a large number of them together.<ref> https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3483613/</ref>",
                    "#Learn [[Use a Multimeter|how a multimeter works]]. Multimeters measure AC and DC voltage, electrical resistance, and the flow of current. For testing a fuse, you can either use it to measure the continuity (which tests if the circuit is complete) or the ohms (which tests resistance). <ref>https://cdn.sparkfun.com/assets/4/6/d/3/b/How_to_Use_a_Multimeter.pdf</ref>[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 2 Version 4.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#*The cartridge fuse is a cylindrical fuse that has been common in a wide variety of devices for many years, from homes to small electronic devices. They have metal contact or terminal points on either end and consist primarily of a tube that contains the wire.<ref>http://electrical.about.com/od/panelsdistribution/a/cartridgefuses.htm</ref>",
                    "#*The blade fuse is a common type of automotive fuse that has come into use in the last 20-30 years. They vaguely resemble the plug of a power cord, with two metal prongs emanating from a plastic housing that contains the wire. Previously, most vehicles also contained small glass cartridge fuses. Blade fuses plug conveniently into banks, and relatively little space is required to house a large number of them together.<ref> http://www.edmunds.com/how-to/how-to-inspect-and-replace-automotive-fuses.html</ref>",
                    "#Learn [[Use a Multimeter|how a multimeter works]]. Multimeters measure AC and DC voltage, electrical resistance, and the flow of current. For testing a fuse, you can either use it to measure the continuity (which tests if the circuit is complete) or the ohms (which tests resistance). <ref>http://www.wisegeek.org/what-is-a-multimeter.htm</ref>[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 2 Version 4.jpg|center]]"
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "30903386",
            "user": "WikiHow Projects",
            "timestamp": "14:10, 23 September 2022",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(6,869 bytes)",
            "change": 130,
            "summary": "(Adding internal links)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Learn [[Use a Multimeter|how a multimeter works]]. Multimeters measure AC and DC voltage, electrical resistance, and the flow of current. For testing a fuse, you can either use it to measure the continuity (which tests if the circuit is complete) or the ohms (which tests resistance). <ref>http://www.wisegeek.org/what-is-a-multimeter.htm</ref>[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 2 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Understand why you must [[Check Fuses|test fuses]]. Testing fuses is the simplest way to examine what is going on in the electrical systems of your car or home, and for that reason, it is a vital skill to possess.[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 3 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Turn the equipment off and remove the fuse. Make sure the device, equipment, or vehicle is turned off before the fuse is removed. To [[Change Fuses in a Car|remove the fuse]], simply pull it straight out of the slot.[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 5 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Turn the meter on and set it to [[Test Continuity with a Multimeter|measure continuity]]. Turn the dial on the multimeter so it points to the continuity setting, which looks like 5 curved vertical lines. Before you test the fuse, put the positive and negative leads together and listen for the meter to beep to ensure it’s working properly.[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 6 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*If you’re using a [[Use a Digital Multimeter|digital multimeter]] set to measure resistance, touch the probes together to get an initial reading. Then put the probes on either side of the fuse and check if the reading is similar. If it is, then the fuse works properly. If you get no reading or “OL”, then the fuse has blown."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Learn how a multimeter works. Multimeters measure AC and DC voltage, electrical resistance, and the flow of current. For testing a fuse, you can either use it to measure the continuity (which tests if the circuit is complete) or the ohms (which tests resistance). <ref>http://www.wisegeek.org/what-is-a-multimeter.htm</ref>[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 2 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Understand why you must test fuses. Testing fuses is the simplest way to examine what is going on in the electrical systems of your car or home, and for that reason, it is a vital skill to possess.[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 3 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Turn the equipment off and remove the fuse. Make sure the device, equipment, or vehicle is turned off before the fuse is removed. To remove the fuse, simply pull it straight out of the slot.[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 5 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Turn the meter on and set it to measure continuity. Turn the dial on the multimeter so it points to the continuity setting, which looks like 5 curved vertical lines. Before you test the fuse, put the positive and negative leads together and listen for the meter to beep to ensure it’s working properly.[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 6 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*If you’re using a digital multimeter set to measure resistance, touch the probes together to get an initial reading. Then put the probes on either side of the fuse and check if the reading is similar. If it is, then the fuse works properly. If you get no reading or “OL”, then the fuse has blown."
                ]
            }
        },
