        {
            "id": "24404770",
            "user": "WikiHow Expert Review",
            "timestamp": "18:56, 18 April 2018",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(6,283 bytes)",
            "change": 75,
            "summary": "(Incorporating expert feedback)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Understand fuses. Fuses are really just wires that are designed not to last, but their purpose is to prevent damage to more valuable electrical equipment or prevent fires (especially in homes) caused by power surges. If too much power runs through the fuse, it will \"burn out,\" quite literally, and open the circuit, preventing the current from flowing through the circuit. There are number of varieties of fuse, but their differences are primarily in appearance. Here is a description of the two you are most likely to see:[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 1 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Turn the equipment off and remove the fuse. Make sure the device, equipment, or vehicle is turned off before the fuse is removed. To remove the fuse, simply pull it straight out of the slot.[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 5 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Turn the meter on and set it. You can rotate the selector to Ω, or OHMS. This will measure resistance. Before you test the fuse, put the positive and negative leads together and look at the reading. The number it provides should be close to the one you see when you test the fuse.[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 6 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Put one lead on each end of the fuse and look at the display. Because the fuse is little more than a single wire—and no complex parts to worry about—it does not matter which side receives the positive or negative lead.[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 7 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Test the fuse. If you’re using the multimeter to measure ohms, the reading should match (or nearly match) the one provided when you touched the two leads. If the fuse is blown, it will not read anything at all, or the meter will display O.L., depending on the brand and type of meter you are using.[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 8.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*If you’re using a digital multimeter set to measure continuity, the meter should beep continuously as you hold the leads to the ends of the fuse. That means the circuit is complete. If it does not do so, the fuse is blown.",
                    "*Never test a fuse on equipment that is still on.",
                    "*Never replace a blown or suspect fuse with one of a higher rating. The rating ensures that the current can pass through the wiring safely. Always replace a fuse with exactly the same rating as (or a lower rating than) the old one."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "",
                    "#Understand fuses. Fuses are really just wires that are designed not to last, but their purpose is to prevent damage to more valuable electrical equipment or prevent fires (especially in homes) caused by power surges. If too much power runs through the fuse, it will \"burn out,\" quite literally, and break the circuit. There are number of varieties of fuse, but their differences are primarily in appearance. Here is a description of the two you are most likely to see:[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 1 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "",
                    "#Turn the device off first and Remove power.[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 4 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Remove the fuse from the vehicle or device. Make sure it is turned off before the fuse is removed.[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 5 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Turn the meter on and set it.[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 6 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*You can rotate the selector to Ω, or OHMS. This will measure resistance. Before you test the fuse, put the positive and negative leads together and look at the reading. The number it provides should be close to the one you see when you test the fuse.",
                    "#Put one lead on each end of the fuse and look at the display.[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 7 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Because the fuse is little more than a single wire--and no complex parts to worry about--it does not matter which side receives the positive or negative lead.",
                    "#Test the fuse.[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 8.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*If using the multimeter to measure ohms, the reading should match (or nearly match) the one provided when you touched the two leads. If the fuse is blown, it will not read anything at all, or the meter will display O.L. depending on the brand and type of meter you are using.",
                    "#*If using a digital multimeter set to measure continuity, the meter should beep continuously as you hold the leads to the ends of the fuse. That means the circuit is complete. If it does not do so, the fuse is blown.",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "*Never test a fuse on a machine that is still on.",
                    "*Never replace a blown or suspect fuse with one of a higher rating. They are rated for safety reasons and you should always replace a fuse with exactly (or sometimes lower) rating as the old one.",
