            "summary": "(Saving new step-by-step photos)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Understand fuses. Fuses are really just wires that are designed not to last, but their purpose is to prevent damage to more valuable electrical equipment or prevent fires (especially in homes) caused by power surges. If too much power runs through the fuse, it will \"burn out,\" quite literally, and break the circuit. There are number of varieties of fuse, but their differences are primarily in appearance. Here is a description of the two you are most likely to see:[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 1 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Learn how a multimeter works. Multimeters measure AC and DC voltage, electrical resistance, and the flow of current. For testing a fuse, you can either use it as an ohmmeter (that is, a meter that tests resistance) or an ammeter (a meter that tests the flow of current). <ref>http://www.wisegeek.org/what-is-a-multimeter.htm</ref>[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 2 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Understand why you must test fuses. Testing fuses is the simplest way to examine what is going on in the electrical systems of your car or home, and for that reason it is a vital skill to possess.[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 3 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Turn the device off first and Remove power.[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 4 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Remove the fuse from the vehicle or device. Make sure it is turned off before the fuse is removed.[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 5 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Turn the meter on and set it.[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 6 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Put one lead on each end of the fuse and look at the display.[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 7 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Test the fuse.[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 8.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Understand fuses. Fuses are really just wires that are designed not to last, but their purpose is to prevent damage to more valuable electrical equipment or prevent fires (especially in homes) caused by power surges. If too much power runs through the fuse, it will \"burn out,\" quite literally, and break the circuit. There are number of varieties of fuse, but their differences are primarily in appearance. Here is a description of the two you are most likely to see:[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 1 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Learn how a multimeter works. Multimeters measure AC and DC voltage, electrical resistance, and the flow of current. For testing a fuse, you can either use it as an ohmmeter (that is, a meter that tests resistance) or an ammeter (a meter that tests the flow of current). <ref>http://www.wisegeek.org/what-is-a-multimeter.htm</ref>[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 2 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Understand why you must test fuses. Testing fuses is the simplest way to examine what is going on in the electrical systems of your car or home, and for that reason it is a vital skill to possess.[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 3 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Turn the device off first and Remove power.",
                    "#Remove the fuse from the vehicle or device. Make sure it is turned off before the fuse is removed.[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 4 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Turn the meter on and set it.[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 5 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Put one lead on each end of the fuse and look at the display.[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 6.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Test the fuse.[[Image:Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 7.jpg|center]]"
                ]
            }
        },
