            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#*The cartridge fuse is a cylindrical fuse that has been common in a wide variety of devices for many years, from homes to small electronic devices. They have metal contact or terminal points on either end and consist primarily of a tube that contains the wire.<ref>http://electrical.about.com/od/panelsdistribution/a/cartridgefuses.htm</ref>",
                    "#*The blade fuse is a common type of automotive fuse that has come into use in the last 20-30 years. They vaguely resemble the plug of a power cord, with two metal prongs emanating from a plastic housing that contains the wire. Previously, most vehicles also contained small glass cartridge fuses. Blade fuses plug conveniently into banks, and relatively little space is required to house a large number of them together.<ref> http://www.edmunds.com/how-to/how-to-inspect-and-replace-automotive-fuses.html</ref>",
                    "#*Alternatively, you can test the flow of current by setting the meter to the symbol that looks like an arrow traveling along a line."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#*The cartridge fuse is a cylindrical fuse that has been common in a wide variety of devices for many years, from homes to small electronic devices. They have metal contact or terminal points on either end and consist primarily of a tube that contains the wire.<ref>http://electrical.about.com/od/panelsdistribution/a/cartridgefuses.htm</ref> [[Image:Glass fuse.jpg|right|181px]]",
                    "#*The blade fuse is a common type of automotive fuse that has come into use in the last 20-30 years. They vaguely resemble the plug of a power cord, with two metal prongs emanating from a plastic housing that contains the wire. Previously, most vehicles also contained small glass cartridge fuses. Blade fuses plug conveniently into banks, and relatively little space is required to house a large number of them together.<ref> http://www.edmunds.com/how-to/how-to-inspect-and-replace-automotive-fuses.html</ref> [[Image:Blade fuse.jpg|right|179px]]",
                    "#*Alternatively, you can test the flow of current by setting the meter to the symbol that looks like an arrow traveling along a line.[[Image:Current.jpg|right|181px]]"
                ]
