        {
            "id": "16164614",
            "user": "Seymour Edits",
            "timestamp": "18:50, 16 December 2014",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(5,961 bytes)",
            "change": 2934,
            "summary": "(Added citations, parts)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "major_addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "Cars and older homes that don’t use modern circuit breakers require testing to check that they are still in good working order. Testing fuses can be done using a multimeter, and doing so is both fast and easy to learn.",
                    "===Part 1:  Learning about Fuses and Multimeters===",
                    "#Understand fuses. Fuses are really just wires that are designed not to last, but their purpose is to save more valuable electrical equipment from power surges. If too much power runs through the fuse, it will \"burn out,\" quite literally, and break the circuit. There are number of varieties of fuse, but their differences are primarily in appearance. Here is a description of the two you are most likely to see:",
                    "#*The cartridge fuse is a cylindrical fuse that has been common in a wide variety of devices for many years, from homes to small electronic devices. They have metal contact or terminal points on either end and consist primarily of a tube that contains the wire.<ref>http://electrical.about.com/od/panelsdistribution/a/cartridgefuses.htm</ref> [[Image:Glass fuse.jpg|right|181px]]",
                    "#*The blade fuse is a common type of automotive fuse that has come into use in the last 20-30 years. They vaguely resemble the plug of a power cord, with two metal prongs emanating from a plastic housing that contains the wire. Previously, most vehicles also contained small glass cartridge fuses. Blade fuses plug conveniently into banks, and relatively little space is required to house a large number of them together.<ref> http://www.edmunds.com/how-to/how-to-inspect-and-replace-automotive-fuses.html</ref> [[Image:Blade fuse.jpg|right|179px]]",
                    "#Learn how a multimeter works. Multimeters measure AC and DC voltage, electrical resistance, and the flow of current. For testing a fuse, you can either use it as an ohmmeter (that is, a meter that tests resistance) or an ammeter (a meter that tests the flow of current). <ref>http://www.wisegeek.org/what-is-a-multimeter.htm</ref>",
                    "#*A multimeter has a positive and negative lead. When testing resistance or current flow in a circuit, the meter will transmit a small quantity of electricity from its own battery and then measure the amount that passes through the circuit or object.",
                    "#Understand why you must test fuses. Many types of fuses allow for visual confirmation that the fuse is still functional. They are made clear so that you can see if the wire remains intact. If the translucent area is blackened, it is '''usually''' because the fuse has burned out. However, some fuses will create that blackened stain after only slightly overheating, and that may have even been the result of a unnoticed incident weeks or months earlier. If a device is not working, you should test the fuses. If they are all still working, there is likely a more serious problem with the device.",
                    "===Part 2:  Testing the Fuse===",
                    "#Remove the fuse from the vehicle or device. Make sure it is turned off before the fuse is removed.<br><br>{{whvid|Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 1.360p.mp4|Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 1-preview.jpg|Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 1 Version 2.jpg}}",
                    "#Turn the meter on and set it. <br><br>{{whvid|Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 3.360p.mp4|Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 3-preview.jpg|Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 3 Version 2.jpg}}",
                    "#*You can rotate the selector to Ω, or OHMS. This will measure resistance. Before you test the fuse, put the positive and negative leads together and look at the reading. The number it provides should be close to the one you see when you test the fuse.",
                    "#*Alternatively, you can test the flow of current by setting the meter to the symbol that looks like an arrow traveling along a line.[[Image:Current.jpg|right|181px]]",
                    "#*Because the fuse is little more than a single wire--and no complex parts to worry about--it does not matter which side receives the positive or negative lead.",
                    "#Test the fuse. <br><br>{{whvid|Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 4.360p.mp4|Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 4-preview.jpg|Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 4 Version 2.jpg}}",
                    "#*If using the multimeter to measure ohms, the reading should match (or nearly match) the one provided when you touched the two leads. If the fuse is blown, it will not read anything at all.",
                    "#*If using a digital multimeter set to measure current, the meter should beep continuously as you hold the leads to the ends of the fuse. That means the circuit is complete. If it does not do so, the fuse is blown.",
                    "#A blown fuse. <br><br>{{whvid|Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 5.360p.mp4|Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 5-preview.jpg|Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 5 Version 2.jpg}}",
                    "*Household installations these days shouldn't really be protected by just fuses. Modern circuit breakers and protective devices are fuse-less and much safer. Consider having an old fuse installation upgraded to modern standards.",
                    "*Never replace a blown or suspect fuse with one of a higher rating. They are rated for safety reasons and you should always replace a fuse with exactly (or sometimes lower) rating as the old one."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "Cars and older homes that don’t use modern circuit breakers require testing to check that they are still in good working order. Testing fuses can be done using a multimeter. A multimeter is an instrument that measures electric current, voltage, and resistance.",
                    "#Remove the fuse from the car or machine. Make sure the circuits are turned off.<br><br>{{whvid|Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 1.360p.mp4|Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 1-preview.jpg|Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 1 Version 2.jpg}}",
                    "#Turn the meter on and rotate the selector to Ω, or OHMS.<br><br>{{whvid|Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 3.360p.mp4|Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 3-preview.jpg|Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 3 Version 2.jpg}}",
                    "#* Make sure your meter's leads are connected to +ve and common.",
                    "#If the display reads full-scale or doesn't change from before you tested the fuse, it has blown.<br><br>{{whvid|Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 4.360p.mp4|Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 4-preview.jpg|Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 4 Version 2.jpg}}",
                    "#If the meter shows a low number (normally less than 1), then the fuse should be okay. Some meters have a 'continuity' selection. This will either beep or change the displayed number if the fuse is good when tested. Or it will remain silent and the number will remain static if the fuse is blown.<br><br>{{whvid|Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 5.360p.mp4|Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 5-preview.jpg|Test a Fuse With a Multimeter Step 5 Version 2.jpg}}",
                    "*Some car fuses are glass tubes (older cars in the main) and it is quite easy to see that the wire inside has broken in two, indicating the fuse has blown without the need for a meter.",
                    "*Similarly, household installations these days shouldn't really be protected by just fuses. Modern circuit breakers and protective devices are fuse-less and much safer. Consider having an old fuse installation upgraded to modern standards.",
                    "*Never replace a blown or suspect fuse with one of a higher rating, they are rated for safety reasons and you should always replace a fuse with exactly (or sometimes lower) rating as the old one."
                ]
            }
        },
